==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[n-]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*ne||not}}, from Proto-Indo-European {{m|ine-pro|*ne||no, not}}. Akin to {{cog|ang|ne||not}}

===Prefix===
{{head|ang|prefix|head=ne-}}

# not, usually syncopated to n-
#: ''{{m|ang|neom||am not}}''
#: ''{{m|ang|næs||was not}}; {{m|ang|nǣron||were not}}''
#: ''{{m|ang|nic||not I}}''
#: ''{{m|ang|nǣniġ||no one, none, not any, no (adj.)}}''
#: ''{{m|ang|nealles||not at all, by no means}}''
#: ''{{m|ang|nefne||unless, except, not even}}''
#: ''{{m|ang|nabban||to have not, be without}}''
#: ''{{m|ang|nāgan||to not owe, not own}}''
#: ''{{m|ang|nǣfre||never}}''
#: ''{{m|ang|nyllan||will not, wish not, be unwilling, refuse}}''
#: ''{{m|ang|nāt||[I] do not know}}, from {{m|ang|nytan||to know not}}''
#: ''{{m|ang|nyten||ignorant}} (from ne + witan)''

